The reduced representation is assigned to the nodes that now define the active boundary of the network. Each node compares its local state with the corresponding reduced entry and applies the replacement rule only where the two diverge. Differences are propagated outward along currently available connections, while links that no longer support the replacement organization are discarded.

As the propagation proceeds, several local distinctions become irrelevant and are merged into common states. Other distinctions are retained where they determine different permitted transitions. The network therefore settles into a configuration with fewer effective states but a sharper separation between admissible and inadmissible changes.

Once no boundary node can produce a permitted change from the reduced representation, the configuration is treated as stable. Its remaining structure records only the constraints needed to maintain the new organization;

The compact representation becomes the active state for the next transition. Its preserved relation determines which distinctions remain admissible: configurations differing only in discarded detail are treated alike, while any change affecting the retained dependency is assigned a different state.

The governing rule is therefore applied to this reduced state rather than to the full visible configuration. It permits a transformation only when the retained relation can be carried forward, and it records any newly introduced dependency in the same compressed form. The resulting configuration may differ substantially in appearance, but its classification is determined by how its preserved structure satisfies the updated rule.

A subsequent observation then separates changes that alter the retained organization from those that merely revise its presentation. Only the former cause a new rule adjustment;

The starting configuration is therefore not recovered as a unique object, but as a set of configurations compatible with the inferred transformation. Differences between members of that set are invisible at the level of the available observation and cannot be resolved without additional constraints.

The transformation likewise specifies only those features required to produce the observation. Any incidental arrangement, ordering, or connection that does not affect that production remains undetermined. Reconstruction should consequently preserve the distinctions supported by the compact representation while treating unsupported relations as absent from the result, rather than restoring them by assumption.

The resulting organization is a constrained reconstruction: each retained element has a warranted role in the chain from representation to configuration, while unresolved alternatives remain separate. Further development can proceed only by adding an independent condition that selects among those alternatives;
